nip">Ariadne is a figure of Greek mythology, daughter of King Minos of Crete, who gave Theseus the thread that guided him out of the Labyrinth after he killed the Minotaur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariane-rocket-family"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ariane-rocket-family.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ariane (rocket family)</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Ariane is a series of European civilian launch vehicles developed for the European Space Agency, first flown in 1979.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arianism"><span class="enc-name">Arianism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Arianism was a Christian doctrine, named after the Alexandrian priest Arius, holding that the Son was created by the Father and not of the same substance.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariapeithes"><span class="enc-name">Ariapeithes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ariapeithes was a king of the Scythians in the first half of the 5th century BCE, known from Herodotus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariarathes-i-of-cappadocia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ariarathes-i-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ariarathes I of Cappadocia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ariarathes I was the Persian satrap of Cappadocia who kept control of the region after Alexander the Great conquered the Achaemenid Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariarathes-ii-of-cappadocia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ariarathes-ii-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ariarathes II of Cappadocia</span><span class="enc-t